## The trap: this is not a DP problem

The obvious framing is `reachable[i] = any earlier j that can reach i`, which is O(n^2) and times out at `n = 100000`. It also hides the structure. The useful observation is much simpler.

## The observation

Because a square's number is a **maximum**, reachability is *contiguous*: if you can reach square `i`, you can reach every square before it too. So the entire state of the problem collapses to one number — the furthest square reachable so far.

That is the whole solution. Walk left to right carrying `reach`:

- If `i > reach`, there is a gap you cannot cross. Stop; the answer is `false`.
- Otherwise square `i` is reachable, so extend: `reach = max(reach, i + a[i])`.

If the walk finishes, the last square was never unreachable.

🛑 The check must come **before** the extension. Testing `i > reach` after updating would use square `i`'s own jump to prove square `i` is reachable, which is circular — and the bug passes the easy cases, because a gap only appears when a zero stalls you.

## Why greedy is safe here

Greedy needs an exchange argument, and this one has a clean one: taking the furthest jump available can never be worse than taking a shorter one, since every square a shorter jump reaches is also reached by the longer one. There is no choice to regret, so there is nothing to search.

## Dry run on `3 2 1 0 4`

| i | a[i] | i > reach? | reach after |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 3 | no | 3 |
| 1 | 2 | no | 3 |
| 2 | 1 | no | 3 |
| 3 | 0 | no | 3 |
| 4 | 4 | **yes** | — |

The three squares after index 0 each promise less than they cost, and the zero at index 3 is the wall. `reach` never passes 3, so index 4 is unreachable.

## Edge cases

- `n = 1` is already finished. The loop runs once with `i = 0`, `0 > 0` is false, and the answer is `true` without any jump.
- A leading `0` with `n > 1` is immediately stuck.
- A zero anywhere else is harmless as long as an earlier square jumps over it.

## Complexity

O(n) time, O(1) extra space beyond the input. One pass, one accumulator.

## The complete program

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> a(n);
    for (long long& x : a) cin >> x;

    long long reach = 0;
    bool ok = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        // 🛑 Checked BEFORE extending. Updating first would let square i's own
        // jump prove that square i is reachable.
        if (i > reach) {
            ok = false;
            break;
        }
        reach = max(reach, (long long)i + a[i]);
    }
    cout << (ok ? "true" : "false") << '\n';
}
```
